// Process.enterBackgroundPriority sets the nice value to 10 and answers what getpriority reads back, so off Windows it
// answers 10, and a second call answers 10 again. With getpriority and setpriority under each other's numbers, as arm64
// Linux had them, the set was a read and the read a set of whatever its third register held, so the answer was 20.
// Under the corpus the harness is at nice 10 already, so this sees the numbers of the calls, not whether the set happens.
// unsupported-targets: x64-windows
// native-only
//> first=10
//> second=10

function main() returns ExitCode
	print("first={Process.enterBackgroundPriority()}\n")
	print("second={Process.enterBackgroundPriority()}\n")
	return 0 as ExitCode
end 'main'
